 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n### Issue with `WITHIN GROUP` Clause in DuckDB Dialect\n\n#### Description\n\nI've encountered an issue with the `WITHIN GROUP` clause in the DuckDB dialect. When attempting to use this clause, the generated SQL does not seem to handle the ordering correctly, leading to unexpected results.\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\nThe SQL generated should correctly incorporate the `WITHIN GROUP` clause with the appropriate ordering, ensuring that the query executes as intended.\n\n#### Actual Behavior\n\nThe SQL generated appears to omit or misplace the ordering part of the `WITHIN GROUP` clause, which results in incorrect query execution.\n\n#### Steps to Reproduce\n\nHere's a simplified example to reproduce the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom sqlglot import parse_one, transpile\n\n# Example SQL using WITHIN GROUP\nsql = \"SELECT PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY column1) FROM table\"\n\n# Transpile using DuckDB dialect\ntranspiled_sql = transpile(sql, read='duckdb', write='duckdb')\n\nprint(transpiled_sql)\n```\n\nThe output should correctly reflect the `WITHIN GROUP` clause with the order, but it does not.\n\n#### System Information\n\n- **Python Version**: 3.10.16\n- **Operating System**: Linux\n- **SQLGlot Version**: [version number if known]\n\nAny insights or suggestions on this would be greatly appreciated!\n</issue>\n",
